It started when I tired of nethack and took a Sporkhack break. When
both of my EXP:30 characters' games started crashing inexplicably, I
tired of Sporkhack and played Slash'em. Then I ascended.

A little disclaimer for those of you new to slash'em: doppelgangers
are rather unbalanced characters in the early/middle game for several
reasons, and the Slash'em late game is not very difficult thanks to
gypsies (translation: free wishes), firearms, and several other
factors. Well, there's a mandatory Demogorgon, but he's *easier* when
mandatory because one can prepare for the confrontation from the
safety of the upstairs.

A level 9 doppelganger can, with great energy cost and some nutrition
cost, become any polymorphable creature, including the level 30
crystal golem with 300 HP along with intrinsic magic resistance,
reflection, and drain resistance. To balance this, a low-level
doppelganger only has partial control over which monsters he can
become. My doppelganger circumvented this restriction.

Early in the game, a blessed scroll of identify revealed my
doppelganger had, from several rings picked up on the ground and one
suspicious 300-zorkmid ring from a ring shop, found rings of polymorph
and polymorph control. A ring of polymorph is *the* most coveted item
for a doppelganger, next to an amulet of unchanging (hello, crystal
golem wizard!)  because it allows for unlimited free polymorphs, each
of which restores 100% of your polymorphed form's HP. Needless to say,
Loser the mind flayer was cruising through the Gnomish Mines, where he
enjoyed many complementary meals of gnome brains.

My early game consisted of hiding behind my pet kitty and abusing the
"sleep" spell (sleeping gnomes make *excellent* kitty treats!). Now, I
was tempted to charge downward as a crystal golem after finding the
rings, but previous experience told me that even a crystal golem would
have trouble without an adequate weapon and decent armor (my AC was a
pitiful -2, certainly insufficient for Slash'em).

Since I started with a spellbook of create monster, I camped on dlvl:4
for Magicbane. Camping took a long, long time, but then, dungeon level
4 is a nice place to camp with plenty of tasty, low-level brains -
oops, too much time as a mind flayer, I guess. Realizing that mind
flayers were rather gross, I spent some time as a Xorn, who could
munch on crude daggers for snacks, and could also phase through walls.
As an added benefit, this Xorn found a luckstone *embedded in the
rock* not far from the altar. Lucky, in several ways. :)

Finally, I got Magicbane, and found a magic lamp on the body of a dead
gray-elf while altar-camping. Then I found a magic marker through an
exhaustive search of the early mine levels. I wished for silver dragon
scale mail rather than reverse-genociding silver dragons, enchanted my
armor and a stack of daggers from the mines, and nurse-danced using a
cursed scroll of genocide produced from the marker. For good measure,
since I *really* didn't want to lose this wizard, I succubus-danced,
summoning one of the demons from a sink on level 1. I actually danced
until reaching level 17 for teleport control, for more good
measure. :)

And *then* I descended.

The first order of business, of course, was to get the Eye. Easy
check. Second order of business was to kill One-eyed-Sam as a giant
Shoggoth and get his highly enchanted speed boots. Easier check, and
Sam's now-defunct black market gave me some really nice toys,
including a magic whistle (several, in fact), a ton of attack wands,
and a bunch of spellbooks. Oh, and my *second* magic marker.

The third order of business was to finish the chaotic quest in order
to kill Vecna, who drops the Hand of Vecna if you're a good little boy
and don't stone or disentegrate him. This artifact gives hungerless
regeneration and half physical damage when carried, and drain
resistance, an essential resistance due to "wands of draining" and
deep dragons, when wielded. But Vecna's a @#$&*#$ to kill, and his
quest monsters are undead, which usually include several purple L. Did
I mention you need an attack spell or +4 weapon to even *hurt* this
guy?

Having had three extremely promising characters die to Vecna, I feel I
didn't overprepare for this venture. First, I polymorphed into a giant
shoggoth and munched on Vecna's lesser ilk, immune to touch-of-death
thanks to the Eye of the Aethiopica even as a hulking, gelatinous
glob. Then I became a wizard and approached Vecna from the safety of
an engraved E-word. A few magic missiles and +7 daggers later, he was
history save for his coveted hand.

I considered descending through Gehennom, but feared Demogorgon, so I
nurse-danced to 300 hit points or so, alchemized/polypiled several
potions of full healing, produced a helm of brilliance through
polypiling, enchanted all my armor, and *then* met Demogorgon. The
encounter wasn't too hard! Once Demogorgon exited the safety of his
abode to sting me, I used a cursed scroll of teleportation to travel
upstairs, so I could comfortably chip away at him from the safety of
an engraved E on the upstairs.

After sacking the castle with magic missile spells, I was faced with a
dreadful dilemma: after finding spellbooks of magic mapping
(Aphrodite's stash), finger of death (lost temple), and most
everything else, I received a wish from the castle throne. Slash'em
had so much loot I had nothing left to wish for, even rings.
So I wished for a pet Solar, an upgraded Archon powerful enough to
kill Rodney himself in two rounds of fighting. Then I lost him from a
levelport trap in the Wyrm caves. *sob*

Time passed. I alchemized potion after potion, finally reaching EXP:30
and 400 HP, with pet crystal and steel golems in tow, and three or so
more figurines of solars packed away for the ascension run to replace
their poor lost brother, all from gypsies. Then I decided enough was
enough and ascended. Really, I didn't even *need* the pets. I could
tame any monster, even the bugaboo level-39 Solar I met on the plane
of air, with a few tries due to my high level, and found the correct
altar on my first try. Turn 77000 or so, 400 HP, 600 power, and three
Solars cutting down everything and its baby sister on Astral. To top
it off, I found six magic markers, only used one wish from my wand of
wishing, neglected to use one Solar figurine, and had an un-rubbed
magic lamp in my pack! Oh, and I ascended with that poor lost Solar.
He was still tame after 10,000 turns or so when I found him  - I don't
know why!

It's lonely up here in the demigod bar. I wish I had used some of
those spare wishes on some pet succubi and nymphs, or stoned and broke
a demon lord for my peaceful rock garden. I guess immortality isn't
all it's cracked up to be, but the journey there was sure fun.



